    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.bracketed, new\n\n### Description\n\nHi echasnovski,  \n\nI have an idea for a new module. What do you think?  \n\n#### New Module:  \n**Mode-based Cycling**  \n\n#### Problem:  \nUsing `[b` and `]b` to cycle through buffers is not ideal because it requires alternating between `]` and `b`. Here's a detailed explanation:  \nhttp://xahlee.info/kbd/repeatable_vs_non-repeatable_keys_commands.html  \n\n#### Solution 1:  \nRemap `alt+b` to `]b`, `alt+B` to `[b`...  \nThis isn't ideal, as the number of keybindings needed will quickly become unmanageable as more cycling modules are added.  \n\n#### Solution 2:  \nUsing Hydra:  \n- https://github.com/abo-abo/hydra  \n- https://github.com/anuvyklack/hydra.nvim  \nWhile Hydra provides a solution, I find it too complex.  \n\n#### Solution 3:  \nIntroduce \"modes\" for cycling.  \n\nFor example, modes could be:  \n```\nbuffer  \nwindow  \ndiagnostic  \n```  \nWhen in **buffer mode**, `move_forward` cycles to the next buffer.  \nWhen in **window mode**, `move_forward` cycles to the next window, and so on.  \n\n##### Workflow:  \n1. Set the mode  \n2. Use `move_forward` / `move_backward`  \n\n##### Benefits:  \n1. `move_forward` / `move_backward` can be mapped to easy-to-repeat keys like `f` and `b`.  \n2. Multiple ways to select a mode:  \n   - Common modes can have direct keybindings (e.g., `mb` for buffer mode, `mw` for window mode).  \n   - Less frequently used modes can be selected via a picker plugin.  \n   This significantly reduces the number of shortcuts to bind and memorize.  \n\n##### Additional Enhancements:  \n1. Display the current mode in the status bar for better visibility.  \n2. Frequently used shortcuts can be further simplified (e.g., `mbf` → `mb` to select buffer mode and move forward in one step).  \n3. Each mode should have two possible \"forward directions\"—if direction is left, `move_forward` moves left; if direction is right, `move_forward` moves right.  \n\n#### related:\n#1077",
