            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\n\nI considered adding this for a while but ended up against it for at least the following reasons:\n- I don't think motivating users to become comfortable with \"spamming\" multiple \"move to next buffer/diagnostic\" in a row is a good idea long term. Those are meant mostly to be used one-two times for a precise jumping (like `2]B` to jump to second-to-last buffer or `]d` to exactly next one) while resorting to fuzzy picking (with `:Pick buffers` or `:Pick diagnostic`) for other cases.\n- These kind of \"modes\" usually need an extra key to explicitly \"end the mode\" (like `<Esc>`). If mode is stopped after any key that is not allowed in the mode, there will inevitably be conflicts. Plus this approach requires emulating the key press afterwards which is not trivial to do robustly, unfortunately.\n- Some/most of this functionality can be done in 'mini.clue' with something like [this example](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/88f18a7d018d8bb4d32f6be4c712134a10e826d3/doc/mini-clue.txt#L413-L435). The one drawback of this particular solution is that it allows repeating only in the same direction: after pressing `]b` it can only move forward by pressing `b`, no backward. But it still seems like an improvement.\n\nSo am afraid I am going to close this as not planned. Thanks for the detailed suggestion, though!\n\n---\n\nNote for the future: there is a Neovim issue neovim/neovim#992 which if implemented (properly) might makethis kind of idea worthwhile.",
